  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will retrograde westward late Saturday, \naccompanied by backdoor cold frontal passage through the CWA \nSaturday evening into night as a surface high pressure center builds \nacross the Great Lakes. Behind the front, northeasterly, low-level \nCAA will be weak but persistent from Sunday into Monday. Although \n850-hPa temperatures will cool in response, they are never really \nprojected to fall below the 90th percentile in most ensemble model \nguidance. Therefore, high temperatures will be cooler, but still \naround 90 to the mid-90s F (10 to 15 F above average). The heat \nindex forecast for Sunday is much more nuanced than preceding days \nwith a corridor of higher pooled dewpoints giving way to a much \ndrier airmass filtering southwestward into the CWA through the day. \nSunday's ensemble model probabilities of 100+ F heat index values \nare highest (40 to 60 percent) in central and southeastern MO where \nCAA will be weakest and dry air arrival latest, but the \naforementioned nuances preclude extension/issuance of another heat \nheadline for Sunday at this time. A more widespread, confident \nrespite from the heat will be on Monday (Labor Day) with 80 to near \n100 percent probabilities of BELOW 100 F heat index values as \ndewpoints lower into the upper 50s to mid-60s F.\n\nTuesday through mid-next week, the upper-level ridge will migrate \nback eastward over the Mid-Mississippi River Valley, further \namplifying to near record-breaking 500-hPa heights. This evolution \nwill establish a similar pattern to the once currently in place with \nanother round of heat Tuesday and Wednesday. In this pattern, high \ntemperatures would again be in the mid-90s to around 100 F, but the \narrival or approach of a cold front on Wednesday decreases \nconfidence in exact values, especially in northeastern MO/west-\ncentral IL.\n\nOn Wednesday through Thursday, global model guidance is beginning to \nbetter align on upper-level trough propagating from the Northern \nPlains through the Great Lakes, which forces the aforementioned \nfront into the Mid-Mississippi River Valley late Wednesday and \nflattens the ridge. Subsequently, it is unclear if the front fully \npasses through the CWA before it wavers with upper-level flow at \nleast briefly becoming quasi-zonal or west-northwesterly as the \nridge potentially retrogrades westward once again. The position of \nthis front will be crucial in determining temperatures, with heat \nfavored to persist to the south/southwest and cooler temperatures, \nperiodic showers/thunderstorms and clouds to the north/ \nnortheast. Unsurprisingly, NBM interquartile temperature ranges\nspan 10 F by that time, albeit still entirely above average for\nmid-September.",
